  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1147 AM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nMid/upper level ridge holds firm over the upper Great Lakes for \nseveral more days given blocking/deep upper lows over New England \nand the southwest CONUS. Associated surface high brings cool/dry \nnortherly flow that persists through Monday. High temps remain in \nthe low/mid 70s with cool morning lows in the mid 40s. High clouds \npossible at times (especially by Tuesday) but overall skies remain \nmostly sunny. Some patchy fog remains possible tonight given mostly \nclear skies and light winds. However, low level flow is backed a bit \nmore northerly tonight which will limit Lake Erie contribution that \nsupported fog/stratus in our SE this morning. The same is true for \nMonday morning as well. Will hold off on mentioning fog for now but \na patchy mention may need to be added.\n\nPattern finally shifts by late Tue as strong Pacific upper jet moves \nacross Canada and forces some progression in the aformentioned \nfeatures. New England upper low finally lifts, replaced by our \npresent ridge. This yields increasing SW flow/theta-e advection into \nthe Upper Midwest ahead of a positively-tilted midlevel trough and \n(increasingly orphaned) surface front. This front brings our first \nchance of rain by Wed, though chances are not high given rapid loss \nof midlevel support. This front stalls over our region as attention \nturns to cutoff low that slowly meanders over the southwest CONUS \nand eventually ejects into the Southern Plains. Models continue to \nstruggle with how this overall pattern evolves and this leads to \nconsiderable uncertainty regarding timing and location of any \nrain/storms along the downstream warm front. Therefore, no changes \nto concensus initialization which has 30-60 PoP's for the entire \nsecond half of the week. Pattern recognition suggests some heavy \nrain potential somewhere in the region but confidence is low \nregarding whether or not it will be in our CWA.",
